To create a base UI library for MAUI projects, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new .NET MAUI library project
First, create a new .NET MAUI library project that will serve as the base UI library.

- Open a terminal or command prompt and navigate to a directory where your projects are stored.
- Install the templates for .NET MAUI if you haven't already done so by running `dotnet new --install Microsoft.Maui.Templates`.
- Execute the following command to create a new .NET MAUI library project:
```sh
dotnet new maui-lib --name YourBaseUILibrary
```
Replace `YourBaseUILibrary` with the desired name for your project. This will create a new folder with the same name and the necessary files for a .NET MAUI library.

2. Create your custom components and styles.
   Inside your `YourBaseUILibrary` project, create custom UI components and styles. For example, if you want to create a custom button, you can create a new C# class that inherits the MAUI Button class and adds your custom functionality/styles.

- Add a new folder named `Controls`
- Inside `Controls` folder, create a new C# file named `CustomButton.cs`
- Add this code to `CustomButton.cs`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls;

namespace YourBaseUILibrary.Controls
{
    public class CustomButton : Button
    {
        // Now you can add your custom functionality and styles to this class.
        public CustomButton()
        {
            // For example, set a default background color:
            BackgroundColor = Color.Red;
        }
    }
}
```

3. Build your library project.
   Build your `YourBaseUILibrary` project to create the library output.

```sh
cd YourBaseUILibrary
dotnet build
```

4. Reference `YourBaseUILibrary` in your MAUI project.
- Open the .csproj file of your main MAUI project in a text editor.
- Add a `ProjectReference` to the `ItemGroup` section like this:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
    <!-- ... -->
    <ProjectReference Include="path/to/YourBaseUILibrary/YourBaseUILibrary.csproj" />
</ItemGroup>
```

5. Use your custom components in your MAUI app.
In your main MAUI project, you can now import and use the custom components you created in `YourBaseUILibrary`. For example, to use the `CustomButton` component:

```csharp
using YourBaseUILibrary.Controls;
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls;

namespace MyApp
{
    public class MainPage : ContentPage
    {
        public MainPage()
        {
            var myCustomButton = new CustomButton()
            {
                Text = "Click me!",
                HorizontalOptions = LayoutOptions.Center,
                VerticalOptions = LayoutOptions.Center
            };

            Content = myCustomButton;
        }
    }
}
```

Now, when you build and run your main MAUI app, you will see your custom button from the base UI library displayed on the screen.

Remember to keep your base UI library up-to-date and distribute it to other projects when you add or update components.

You can transfer a float to XMVECTOR in C++ using the following code:

```
#include <DirectXMath.h>

// assuming 'myFloat' contains the float value you want to transfer

