   Replace `https://api.url/your-api` with a proper API source to search for Elon Musk's quotes. You can use an external API or create your own Elon Musk quotes database by scraping quotes from a reliable source.

4. Create a content script:

   a. In your extension's directory, create a new JavaScript file named `content.js`.

   b. Add the following code to the `content.js`:

```js
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((request) => {
  if (request.action === "showQuote") {
    const elonQuoteBox = createQuoteBox(request.quote);
    document.body.appendChild(elonQuoteBox);
  }
});

function createQuoteBox(quote) {
  const quoteBox = document.createElement("div");
  quoteBox.style.position = "fixed";
  quoteBox.style.zIndex = 9999;
  quoteBox.style.backgroundColor = "#FFFFFF";
  quoteBox.style.padding = "20px";
  quoteBox.style.border = "2px solid #000000";
  quoteBox.style.borderRadius = "5px";
  quoteBox.style.top = "50%";
  quoteBox.style.left = "50%";
  quoteBox.style.transform = "translate(-50%, -50%)";
  quoteBox.innerHTML = `<p style="font-weight:bold;">Elon Musk Quote:</p><p>${quote}</p>`;

  const closeButton = document.createElement("button");
  closeButton.style.position = "absolute";
  closeButton.style.top = "5px";
  closeButton.style.right = "5px";
  closeButton.innerHTML = "X";

  closeButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
    quoteBox.remove();
  });

  quoteBox.appendChild(closeButton);
  return quoteBox;
}
```

5. Update your manifest:

   Add content scripts to your `manifest.json`:

```json
"content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["*://*/*"],
      "js": ["content.js"]
    }
  ]
```

6. Add an icon:

   a. Create a 128x128 pixel icon and save it as `icon128.png` in your extension's directory.

7. Load and test your extension:

