In those transactions where there is no closed order, you need to display real-time candles on the chart and calculate the percentage at the current price. This is necessary so that you can follow the transaction in real time. It is necessary that the chart reflects the current situation on the market, as here https://www.binance.com/ru/futures/DOGEUSDT

Here's an example of how you can display real-time candles and calculate the percentage at the current price using Binance API:

1. Create a new Context for Binance API connection.

Create a new file `BinanceContext.tsx`:

```javascript
import React, { createContext, useContext, useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { w3cwebsocket as W3CWebSocket } from "websocket";

const BinanceContext = createContext<{
  socket: W3CWebSocket | null;
  setSocket: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<W3CWebSocket | null>>;
}>({
  socket: null,
  setSocket: () => {},
});

export const BinanceProvider: React.FC = ({ children }) => {
  const [socket, setSocket] = useState<W3CWebSocket | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const newSocket = new W3CWebSocket(
      "wss://stream.binance.com:9443/ws/dogeusdt@kline_1m"
    );
    setSocket(newSocket);

    newSocket.onerror = () => {
      console.log("Connection Error");
    };

    newSocket.onclose = () => {
      console.log("WebSocket Connection Closed");
    };

    return () => {
      newSocket.close();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <BinanceContext.Provider value={{ socket, setSocket }}>
      {children}
    </BinanceContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useBinanceContext = () => useContext(BinanceContext);
```

2. Wrap your App component in BinanceProvider.

In your main App.tsx (or whatever your main component is):

```javascript
import { BinanceProvider } from "./components/BinanceContext";

export default function App() {
  return (
    <BinanceProvider>
      {/* other components */}
    </BinanceProvider>
  );
}
```

3. Modify your `TradeDetails` component to listen for real-time updates.

Update your `TradeDetails.tsx` to use `useBinanceContext` and update the candles with real-time data:

```javascript
import { useBinanceContext } from "./BinanceContext";

const TradeDetails = ({ tradeId, defaultChartInterval }: TradeDetailsProps) => {
  // ... previous code

  const { socket } = useBinanceContext();

  useEffect(() => {
    if (socket) {
      socket.onmessage = (message) => {
        const data = JSON.parse(message.data);
        if (data?.k) {
          const kline = data.k;
          const newCandle: CandlePrice = {
            timestamp: kline.t,
            open: kline.o,
            high: kline.h,
            low: kline.l,
            close: kline.c,
            volume: kline.v,
          };
          setCandleData((prevData) => [...prevData, newCandle]);
        }
      };
    }
  }, [socket]);

  // ... rest of the code
};
```

Now, you should have real-time updates on the chart. Additionally, you can use the real-time data to calculate the percentage at the current price and display it on your chart's UI.

Keep in mind that this example uses the Binance API to fetch real-time data for the DOGEUSDT pair at a 1-minute interval. You can adjust this to your needs. Also, remember to handle possible WebSocket errors and reconnections in a production environment.

