To introduce shading into a 3D game written in C# and Monogame, you can follow these steps:

1. Create shaders:
First, you need to create shader files to handle shading in your game. In Monogame, shaders are written in HLSL (High-Level Shader Language).

Create two separate files:
- VertexShader.hlsl: For vertex shader responsible for processing the 3D model's vertices and passing the data to the pixel shader.
- PixelShader.hlsl: For pixel shader responsible for calculating the final color of each pixel on the screen based on the lights, textures, and other factors.

2. Compile shaders:
To use shader files in Monogame, you need to compile them using the Monogame Pipeline Tool. Add both VertexShader.hlsl and PixelShader.hlsl to the Content folder in your Monogame project, set the Build Action property to Compile, and set their respective types: Vertex Shader and Pixel Shader.

3. Load shaders in your game:
In your 3D game project, load the compiled shaders to use them for rendering objects.

```csharp
Effect shaderEffect;
protected override void LoadContent()
{
    shaderEffect = Content.Load<Effect>("shaderEffect"); // Name of your compiled shader effect
}
```

4. Define lighting parameters:
Define necessary lighting parameters like light direction, color, and intensity. You can store these values in a Vector3 or similar object, and pass them to the shader later.

```csharp
Vector3 lightDirection = new Vector3(1, -1, 1);
Color ambientColor = Color.Gray;
Vector3 diffuseColor = Color.White.ToVector3();
float ambientIntensity = 0.1f;
```

5. Set up data for shaders:
Before drawing your 3D objects, set up your shaders with the necessary data like the World, View, and Projection matrices, as well as lighting parameters.

```csharp
shaderEffect.Parameters["World"].SetValue(worldMatrix);
shaderEffect.Parameters["View"].SetValue(viewMatrix);
shaderEffect.Parameters["Projection"].SetValue(projectionMatrix);

shaderEffect.Parameters["LightDirection"].SetValue(lightDirection);
shaderEffect.Parameters["AmbientColor"].SetValue(ambientColor.ToVector4());
shaderEffect.Parameters["DiffuseColor"].SetValue(diffuseColor);
shaderEffect.Parameters["AmbientIntensity"].SetValue(ambientIntensity);
```

6. Apply shaders when rendering:
To use the custom shading for drawing your 3D objects, apply the shader when rendering. Iterate through shader passes, set the active technique, and draw the model.

```csharp
foreach (ModelMesh mesh in model.Meshes)
{
    foreach (ModelMeshPart part in mesh.MeshParts)
    {
        part.Effect = shaderEffect;
    }

    mesh.Draw();
}
```

This will introduce shading to your 3D game using custom shaders in Monogame. Remember to tweak shader code and lighting parameters to achieve desired shading style and effects.

For this example, let's create a simple Vue.js application with Vue Router and transitions between different routes. We will create a simple Home and About page and animate the transition between these two routes. Make sure you have Node.js and npm installed on your system to follow this example.

1. First, install the Vue CLI globally:

```bash
npm install -g @vue/cli
```

2. Create a new Vue.js project:

```bash
vue create vue-router-transition-example
```

Select the default preset and go to the newly created project directory:

```bash
cd vue-router-transition-example
```

3. Add Vue Router to the project:

```bash
vue add router
```

Choose "Yes" when asked if you want to use history mode.

4. Now, let's edit the `src/views/Home.vue` file to change its content. Replace its content with the following code:

```html
<template>
  <div class="home">
    <h1>Home</h1>
    <p>This is the home page</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: "Home",
};
</script>
```

5. Similarly, edit the `src/views/About.vue` file:

```html
<template>
  <div class="about">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>This is the about page</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: "About",
};
</script>
```

6. Next, edit the router by opening `src/router/index.js` file:

```javascript
import { createRouter, createWebHistory } from "vue-router";
import Home from "../views/Home.vue";
import About from "../views/About.vue";

const routes = [
  {
    path: "/",
    name: "Home",
    component: Home,
  },
  {
    path: "/about",
    name: "About",
    component: About,
  },
];

const router = createRouter({
  history: createWebHistory(process.env.BASE_URL),
  routes,
});

export default router;
```

7. Now, let's add a transition to the route views. Edit the `src/App.vue` file:

```html
<template>
  <div id="app">
    <div id="nav">
      <router-link to="/">Home</router-link> |
      <router-link to="/about">About</router-link>
    </div>
    <transition name="fade">
      <router-view/>
    </transition>
  </div>
</template>

<style>
#app {
  font-family: Avenir, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
}

#nav {
  padding: 30px;
}

#nav a {
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #2c3e50;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.fade-enter-active,
.fade-leave-active {
  transition: opacity 0.5s;
}

.fade-enter, .fade-leave-to {
  opacity: 0;
}
</style>
```

In this step, we've wrapped the `router-view` component with a `transition` component and added CSS for the fade transition effect.

8. Now, your project is set up and ready to experience route transitions. Start the development server by running:

```bash
npm run serve
```

9. Open your browser and go to `http://localhost:8080`. You should see the Home and About links in the navbar, and when you navigate between these routes, you'll experience a simple fade transition.

The frosted glass effect can be achieved using the `backdrop-filter` CSS property. Note that this property may not work in all browsers, but it has good support in most modern browsers. Here's a simple example of a frosted glass effect using a CSS class:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <style>
    body {
      background-image: url('https://source.unsplash.com/random');
      background-size: cover;
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    }

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      min-height: 100vh;
      padding: 50px;
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }

    .frosted-glass {
      padding: 25px;
      max-width: 400px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
      backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px); /* For better browser compatibility */
      box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
      text-align: center;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="frosted-glass">
      <h2>Frosted Glass Effect</h2>
      <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Nullam in dictum leo, ut lacinia justo. Vivamus porttitor elit et tortor gravida, in maximus ligula varius. Phasellus interdum urna sit amet.
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

This example will create a frosted glass effect on a div element with the `frosted-glass` class. You can replace the background-image URL with any other image of your choice. The `backdrop-filter: blur(10px);` and `-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px);` lines are responsible for creating the frosted glass effect.

