</html>
```

You can also refer to this guide on [How to Separate PHP and HTML Properly](https://www.codementor.io/@meghna83/how-to-separate-php-and-html-properly-m2772mo2c).

2. Use modern frontend tools and libraries: You are currently using plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript without using any frontend frameworks or libraries. You can use modern frontend tools and libraries like React, Angular, Vue, or even Bootstrap to make your project more modern, fast, and user-friendly.

3. Implement a proper session management: Currently, you're initiating sessions but not managing them properly. Make sure to store user data in session after successful login, and create a proper authentication system to identify users before they can access user-specific pages.

4. Use prepared statement for all database queries: In your `user_page.php` file, you have included `database.php` but not used prepared statements. Make sure to always use prepared statements for all database queries to avoid SQL injection vulnerabilities.

5. Implement a proper logout functionality by destroying the user session when the user clicks on the "Log Out" button.

6. Validate user input: Before processing any form input, ensure that the input data is valid and doesn't contain any special characters that might cause security issues or unexpected behavior.

In summary, you have a basic structure, but there's room for improvement in terms of code organization, frontend design, user experience, session management, and security. Focus on these aspects to enhance your project. Keep coding and good luck with your project!

Creating a webview app for Android involves several steps. Follow the instructions below to create a webview app from scratch.

Step 1: Create a new Android Studio project
1. Open Android Studio and click on 'Start a new Android Studio project.'
2. Select 'Empty Activity' and click 'Next.'
3. Enter the application name and package name.
4. Select the 'Language' as 'Java' and choose the 'Minimum API level' as per your requirement. For example, API 19: Android 4.4 (KitKat).
5. Click on 'Finish.'

Step 2: Add internet permission in the AndroidManifest.xml file
Open the AndroidManifest.xml file and add the following line inside the `<manifest>` element:

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
```

Step 3: Add a WebView in the activity_main.xml layout file
Open the activity_main.xml file and insert the following code inside the `<RelativeLayout>`:

```xml
<WebView
    android:id="@+id/web_view"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent" />
```

Step 4: Add the required code in the MainActivity.java file

Replace the existing "MainActivity" with the following code:

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.webkit.WebSettings;
import android.webkit.WebView;
import android.webkit.WebViewClient;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private WebView webView;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);
        webView = (WebView) findViewById(R.id.web_view);

        WebSettings webSettings = webView.getSettings();
        webSettings.setJavaScriptEnabled(true);

        webView.setWebViewClient(new WebViewClient());

        webView.loadUrl("https://www.example.com");
    }

    @Override
    public void onBackPressed() {
        if (webView.canGoBack()) {
            webView.goBack();
        } else {
            super.onBackPressed();
        }
    }
}
```
Replace 'https://www.example.com' with your desired website URL.

Step 5: Running the app
1. Connect your Android device to your computer or use an emulator.
2. Select the target device from the device drop-down menu in the Android Studio toolbar.
3. Click on the 'Run' button (or press Shift + F10) to run the app.

Your webview app should now display the website URL specified in the MainActivity.java file.

Optional (Step 6): Handling SSL Error

Incase your website uses HTTPS and you want to handle SSL error, add following code snippet in MainActivity.java.

```java
import android.webkit.SslErrorHandler;
import android.net.http.SslError;
```

Add this inside the "onCreate" method.

```java
webView.setWebViewClient(new WebViewClient() {
    @Override
    public void onReceivedSslError(WebView view, SslErrorHandler handler, SslError error) {
        handler.proceed(); // Ignore SSL certificate errors
    }
});
```

This code will ignore SSL certificate errors, but using this method is not suggested for production apps due to security reasons. To properly handle SSL certificate errors, refer to the Android Developers documentation on 'WebSettings'.

To create a website like ytmp3.plus, you'll need to use a server-side language to run youtube-dl-js or an equivalent library for downloading and converting YouTube videos. In this example, we'll use Node.js with the `youtube-dl-exec` library. Here's a step-by-step guide to set up a simple website for YouTube video conversion:

1. Install Node.js

Download and install Node.js from the official website: https://nodejs.org/

2. Set up your project

Create a new folder (e.g., "youtube-dl-web") and navigate to it in your terminal or command prompt. Initialize the project by running:

```
npm init -y
```

3. Install dependencies

Install "express", "ejs" (a template engine), and "youtube-dl-exec" by running:

```
npm install express ejs youtube-dl-exec
```

4. Create files and folders

Create an "app.js" file and a "views" folder inside your project folder. Then, create an "index.ejs" file inside the "views" folder:

```
touch app.js
mkdir views
touch views/index.ejs
```

5. Set up your server

Edit the "app.js" file to set up an Express server with an EJS template engine:

```javascript
const express = require("express");
const app = express();
const path = require("path");

app.set("view engine", "ejs");
app.set("views", path.join(__dirname, "views"));

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  res.render("index");
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server started on port ${PORT}`));
```

