Unfortunately, loading a local file directly in a userscript is not possible due to the security restrictions imposed by browsers.

However, you can circumvent this limitation by serving the file using a local web server, and making a request to load the contents from that local web server in your userscript.

Here's how you can modify the userscript to achieve this:

1. Install a simple web server like `http-server` via npm (Node.js' package manager):

```
npm install -g http-server
```

2. Create the `targetWords.json` file to store the targetWords object in json format. Place this file in any directory you want to serve via the local web server.

Example of `targetWords.json`:

```json
{
  "red": [
      "word1",
      "word2"
  ],
  "deepskyblue": [
      "word3",
      "word4"
  ],
  "yellow": [
      "word5",
      "word6"
  ],
  "lawngreen": [
      "word7",
      "word8"
  ]
}
```

3. Run the local web server in the directory containing the `targetWords.json` file:

```
http-server
```

This will start a server on `http://localhost:8080` (or any other available port if 8080 is occupied).

4. Now, modify your userscript to load the targetWords from the local web server:

```js
// ==UserScript==
// @name        markeks
// @namespace   markeks_text
// @match       *://iframe-toloka.com/*
// @grant       GM_xmlhttpRequest
// @version     1.1
// @run-at      document-start
// @description 20/03/2023, 8:11:44 PM
// ==/UserScript==

(function () {
    'use strict';

    fetchTargetWords().then((targetWords) => {
        // ... your existing userscript code ...
    });

    async function fetchTargetWords() {
        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            GM_xmlhttpRequest({
                method: "GET",
                url: "http://localhost:8080/targetWords.json",
                onload: function(response) {
                    const targetWords = JSON.parse(response.responseText);
                    resolve(targetWords);
                }
            });
        });
    }

    // ... your existing userscript code ...

})();
```

This modified userscript will fetch the `targetWords` object from the `targetWords.json` file served by the local web server, and execute the rest of the userscript using the fetched targetWords object.

Make sure to run the local web server and reload the userscript whenever you make any changes to the `targetWords.json` file.

