The executionAsyncId() method of the async_hooks module in Node.js returns the execution ID of the current execution context. This can be useful for tracing asynchronous operations and understanding the order in which they occur. 

The execution ID is a unique identifier that is assigned to a chain of asynchronous function calls. It allows you to trace the flow of execution across different parts of your application, and identify patterns that might be causing performance problems or other issues.

Here is an example usage of the executionAsyncId() method:

```javascript
const async_hooks = require('async_hooks');

function logExecutionId() {
  const executionId = async_hooks.executionAsyncId();
  console.log(`Execution ID: ${executionId}`);
}

logExecutionId();
setTimeout(logExecutionId, 1000);
```

In this example, the logExecutionId() function logs the current execution ID to the console. We call it immediately and also schedule it to run after a delay using setTimeout(). When this code runs, you should see two different execution IDs logged to the console, indicating that the calls are happening in different execution contexts.

