To employ an AMD GPU with Java for processing, you can use a library like Aparapi, which allows Java developers to write code that executes on an AMD_GPU using a Java Thread Pool Executor to distribute tasks across the AMP GPU processing units. Aparapi provides a JavaScript like programming model as an alternative to OpenCL for parallel programming of AMD GPUs (Graphic Processing Units) within Java applications.

Here is an example that employs Aparapi to run a simple kernel on an AMD GPU using a ThreadPoolExecutor with 10 threads:

First, download Aparapi from https://github.com/aparapi/aparapi and follow the instructions to include the JARs in your project.

After obtaining the necessary libraries, you can create a simple example:

```java
import com.aparapi.Kernel;
import com.aparapi.Range;
import com.aparapi.internal.execution.ThreadPoolExecutorMode;

import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor;

public class AparapiThreadPoolExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int size = 1024;
        final float[] a = new float[size];
        final float[] b = new float[size];
        final float[] c = new float[size];

        // Initialize input arrays with random values
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            a[i] = (float) (Math.random() * 100);
            b[i] = (float) (Math.random() * 100);
        }

        ThreadPoolExecutor threadPool = (ThreadPoolExecutor) Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        ThreadPoolExecutorMode.setThreadPool(threadPool);

        Kernel kernel = new Kernel() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                int gid = getGlobalId();
                c[gid] = a[gid] + b[gid];
            }
        };

        kernel.setExplicit(true);
        kernel.put(a);
        kernel.put(b);

        Range range = Range.create(size);
        kernel.execute(range);

        kernel.get(c);

        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            System.out.println("c[" + i + "] = " + c[i]);
        }

        kernel.dispose();
        threadPool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

This example runs a kernel on the AMD GPU that adds corresponding elements of two input float arrays (a and b) and stores the result in an output float array (c). It uses a ThreadPoolExecutor with 10 threads to distribute tasks across the AMD GPU shader.

It's important to note that while this example demonstrates the usage of the ThreadPoolExecutor with Aparapi, you will find that threading makes more sense when handling multiple independent tasks, while GPUs work best on homogeneous data processed in parallel. Even with thread pools, the real-world GPU programming would use all available processing units on the target device rather than a specified number of threads like in the example above.

For more information about Aparapi, visit its official documentation: https://github.com/aparapi/aparapi

