Creating a GUI to display all the available Java classes and their methods on demand would require using reflection, which allows for the inspection and manipulation of classes and objects during runtime. In addition, you could use `Swing` components to create the user interface.

Here's a sample code to achieve this:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.ActionEvent;
import java.awt.event.ActionListener;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.net.URL;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Enumeration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

public class JavaClassesGui {
    private static JComboBox<String> classesComboBox;
    private static JTextArea methodsTextArea;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> {
            try {
                createAndShowGUI();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        });
    }

    private static void createAndShowGUI() throws Exception {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Java Classes and Methods");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setSize(800, 400);

        Container contentPane = frame.getContentPane();
        contentPane.setLayout(new BorderLayout());

        // get classes to load
        List<Class<?>> classes = getClasses("java.lang");
        String[] classNames = classes.stream().map(Class::getName).sorted().toArray(String[]::new);

        // combo box to hold class names
        classesComboBox = new JComboBox<>(classNames);
        contentPane.add(classesComboBox, BorderLayout.NORTH);

        // text area to show methods
        methodsTextArea = new JTextArea();
        methodsTextArea.setEditable(false);
        contentPane.add(new JScrollPane(methodsTextArea), BorderLayout.CENTER);

        // button to request methods on click
        JButton showMethodsButton = new JButton("Show Methods");
        showMethodsButton.addActionListener(showMethodsActionListener());
        contentPane.add(showMethodsButton, BorderLayout.SOUTH);

        frame.setVisible(true);
    }

    private static ActionListener showMethodsActionListener() {
        return (ActionEvent event) -> {
            String className = Objects.requireNonNull(classesComboBox.getSelectedItem()).toString();
            try {
                Class<?> clazz = Class.forName(className);

                Method[] methods = clazz.getDeclaredMethods();
                StringBuilder methodsText = new StringBuilder();

                for (Method method : methods) {
                    methodsText.append(method.toString()).append("\n");
                }

                methodsTextArea.setText(methodsText.toString());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(null, "Unable to retrieve methods for " + className, "Error", JOptionPane.ERROR_MESSAGE);
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        };
    }

    private static List<Class<?>> getClasses(String packageName) throws ClassNotFoundException, IOException {
        ClassLoader classLoader = Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader();
        assert classLoader != null;

        String path = packageName.replace('.', '/');
        Enumeration<URL> resources = classLoader.getResources(path);
        List<File> dirs = new ArrayList<>();

        while (resources.hasMoreElements()) {
            URL resource = resources.nextElement();
            dirs.add(new File(resource.getFile()));
        }

        List<Class<?>> classes = new ArrayList<>();
        for (File directory : dirs) {
            classes.addAll(findClasses(directory, packageName));
        }

        return classes;
    }

    private static List<Class<?>> findClasses(File directory, String packageName) throws ClassNotFoundException {
        List<Class<?>> classes = new ArrayList<>();

        if (!directory.exists()) {
            return classes;
        }

        File[] files = directory.listFiles();
        for (File file : files) {
            String fileName = file.getName();
            if (file.isDirectory()) {
                assert !fileName.contains(".");
                classes.addAll(findClasses(file, packageName + "." + fileName));
            } else if (fileName.endsWith(".class") && !fileName.contains("$")) {
                Class<?> clazz = Class.forName(packageName + '.' + fileName.substring(0, fileName.length() - 6));
                classes.add(clazz);
            }
        }

        return classes;
    }
}
```

In this example, the Java classes from `java.lang` package are displayed in a `JComboBox`, and their methods can be shown in a `JTextArea` by clicking the "Show Methods" button. Note that this code only works for Java standard library classes. Loading classes from external libraries or custom packages requires more consideration, such as specifying the classpath or using custom class loaders.

pthread_attr_setschedpolicy and sched_setscheduler are two different functions used in Linux for setting the scheduling policy of threads. The main difference between them is that pthread_attr_setschedpolicy is used to set the scheduling policy for a thread attribute object, while sched_setscheduler is used to set the scheduling policy for a running thread.

pthread_attr_setschedpolicy:
pthread_attr_setschedpolicy is a function that sets the scheduling policy attribute value of the thread attribute object referenced by the attr parameter. This attribute value will be used by the pthread_create function when a new thread is created. The scheduling policy determines how the operating system schedules a thread to run on the CPU.

sched_setscheduler:
sched_setscheduler is a function that sets the scheduling policy and scheduling parameters for a running thread. The parameters are specified in the sched_param structure. The scheduling policy and parameters determine how the operating system schedules the thread to run on the CPU.

Example:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sched.h>

void *thread_func(void *arg) {
    // thread code
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread;
    pthread_attr_t attr; 

    // Create thread attribute object
    pthread_attr_init(&attr);

    // Set scheduling policy for attribute object
    pthread_attr_setschedpolicy(&attr, SCHED_FIFO);

    // Create thread with attribute
    pthread_create(&thread, &attr, thread_func, NULL);

    // Set scheduling policy for running thread
    struct sched_param param;
    param.sched_priority = 1;
    sched_setscheduler(thread, SCHED_FIFO, &param);

    // Wait for thread to finish
    pthread_join(thread, NULL);

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, we first create a thread attribute object and set its scheduling policy using pthread_attr_setschedpolicy. Then we create a new thread using this attribute object. Later, we set the scheduling policy and parameters for the running thread using sched_setscheduler. Finally, we wait for the thread to finish using pthread_join. This example shows how to use both functions to set the scheduling policy for a thread.

