The code seems to be making an assumption that the input array is sorted, but the problem statement does not mention that the input array will be sorted. Hence in some cases, the code won't be able to find all non-decreasing subsequences. As a result, the 'addAll()' function is called only when the input subarray is non-decreasing, which is incorrect.

To fix this issue, you need to make sure that 'addAll()' is called for all subarrays, and you should check whether the current element being added to the list results in a non-decreasing subsequence or not. Here's the corrected code:

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> findSubsequences(int[] nums) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        HashSet<String> set = new HashSet<>();

        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

        addAll(nums, 0, list, ans, set);
        System.out.println(ans.size());
        return ans;
    }

    private String stringify(List<Integer> list){
        StringBuilder str = new StringBuilder();
        for(int i : list) {
            str.append(i);
            str.append('|');
        }
        return String.valueOf(str);
    }

    private void addAll(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> list, List<List<Integer>> ans, HashSet<String> set) {
        if (start >= nums.length) {
            String stringified = stringify(list);
            if (list.size() >= 2 && !set.contains(stringified)) {
                ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
                set.add(stringified);
            }
            return;
        }

        // Include the current element if it maintains the non-decreasing order.
        if (list.isEmpty() || list.get(list.size() - 1) <= nums[start]) {
            list.add(nums[start]);
            addAll(nums, start + 1, list, ans, set);
            list.remove(list.size() - 1);
        }

        // Exclude the current element and move forward.
        addAll(nums, start + 1, list, ans, set);
    }
}
```

Now the function 'addAll()' will generate all possible combinations and add elements to the list only if it maintains the non-decreasing order. This way, the code will work for both sorted and unsorted input arrays.

