Here is an example Java code for a class browser with a combo box that lists all available JDK classes:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;
import java.lang.reflect.*;

public class ClassBrowser extends JFrame implements ActionListener {
    private JComboBox<String> classList;
    private JTextArea classInfo;

    public ClassBrowser() {
        super("Java Class Browser");
        setDefaultCloseOperation(WindowConstants.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        classList = new JComboBox<>(getAllClassNames());
        classList.addActionListener(this);

        classInfo = new JTextArea(10, 40);
        classInfo.setEditable(false);

        JPanel panel = new JPanel(new BorderLayout());
        panel.add(new JScrollPane(classInfo), BorderLayout.CENTER);
        panel.add(classList, BorderLayout.NORTH);

        setContentPane(panel);
        pack();
        setLocationRelativeTo(null);
    }

    private String[] getAllClassNames() {
        Package[] packages = Package.getPackages();
        String[] classNames = new String[0];
        for (Package pkg : packages) {
            try {
                Class<?>[] classes = Class.forName(pkg.getName().concat(".*")).getClasses();
                String[] names = new String[classes.length];
                for (int i = 0; i < classes.length; i++)
                    names[i] = classes[i].getSimpleName();

                classNames = concat(classNames, names);
            } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
                // This should not happen, ignore
            }
        }
        return classNames;
    }

    private String[] concat(String[] a, String[] b) {
        String[] result = new String[a.length + b.length];
        System.arraycopy(a, 0, result, 0, a.length);
        System.arraycopy(b, 0, result, a.length, b.length);
        return result;
    }

    @Override
    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
        String className = (String) classList.getSelectedItem();
        if (className != null) {
            try {
                Class<?> cls = Class.forName(className);
                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                sb.append(String.format("Class: %s%n", cls.getName()));

                Field[] fields = cls.getFields();
                if (fields.length > 0) {
                    sb.append("Fields:\n");
                    for (Field field : fields)
                        sb.append(String.format("  %s %s%n", field.getType().getSimpleName(), field.getName()));
                }

                Method[] methods = cls.getMethods();
                if (methods.length > 0) {
                    sb.append("Methods:\n");
                    for (Method method : methods) {
                        sb.append(String.format("  %s %s(", method.getReturnType().getSimpleName(), method.getName()));
                        Class<?>[] paramTypes = method.getParameterTypes();
                        for (int i = 0; i < paramTypes.length; i++) {
                            sb.append(String.format("%s var%d", paramTypes[i].getSimpleName(), i));
                            if (i < paramTypes.length - 1)
                                sb.append(", ");
                        }
                        sb.append(")\n");
                    }
                }

                classInfo.setText(sb.toString());
            } catch (ClassNotFoundException ex) {
                classInfo.setText(String.format("%s not found%n", className));
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> new ClassBrowser().setVisible(true));
    }
}
```

In this code, the `getAllClassNames` method uses reflection to find all classes available in the current JVM's classpath, then constructs an array of their simple names. The `classList` combo box is populated with these names. 

When the user selects a class from the combo box, the `actionPerformed` method uses reflection again to obtain the selected class object, and then retrieves information about its fields and methods. This information is formatted into a string and displayed in the `classInfo` text area. 

Note that this code only displays public fields and methods of the selected class, and does not handle inner classes or interfaces.

