Creating a fully functional drag and drop web page elements editor from scratch requires significant work and goes beyond the scope of a text-based response. However, I can provide you with a simple example that demonstrates the basic functionality of drag and drop elements. You can use this example as a starting point and build upon it to create a more advanced editor.

Here's the example with basic drag and drop functionality using plain HTML, JavaScript, and CSS:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Simple Drag and Drop Editor</title>
<style>
  .container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px;
  }
  .elements, .canvas {
    width: 200px;
    min-height: 300px;
    border: 1px solid black;
    padding: 10px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  .element {
    cursor: move;
    user-select: none;
  }
  .canvas {
    position: relative;
  }
  .canvas .element {
    position: absolute;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="container">
  <div class="elements">
    <div class="element" draggable="true">Element 1</div>
    <div class="element" draggable="true">Element 2</div>
    <div class="element" draggable="true">Element 3</div>
  </div>
  <div class="canvas">
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const elements = document.querySelectorAll('.element');
    const canvas = document.querySelector('.canvas');

    elements.forEach((element) => {
      element.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
        e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', e.target.id);
      });
    });

    canvas.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
    });

    canvas.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const id = e.dataTransfer.getData('text');
      const draggableElement = document.getElementById(id);
      const clonedElement = draggableElement.cloneNode(true);

      clonedElement.id = '';
      clonedElement.style.left = `${e.clientX - e.target.getBoundingClientRect().left}px`;
      clonedElement.style.top = `${e.clientY - e.target.getBoundingClientRect().top}px`;

      canvas.appendChild(clonedElement);
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

This simple drag and drop editor features a container with two sections. The left section (`.elements`) contains draggable elements, and the right section (`.canvas`) is where users can drop those elements.

The JavaScript code listens to events related to drag-and-drop, such as `dragstart`, `dragover`, and `drop`. When an element is dropped inside the canvas, it's cloned and appended to the canvas at the desired position.

