To implement a delete messages command, you can add the following code snippet after the initial condition checks in the `messageCreate` event handler. The added code will listen for the `!delete` command with an argument for the number of messages to delete:

```javascript
client.on('messageCreate', async (message) => {
  if (message.author.bot) return;
  if (message.channel.id !== process.env['ch_id']) return;
  if (message.content.startsWith('!')) {
    // Add this code block
    const args = message.content.split(' ');
    const command = args.shift().toLowerCase();

    if (command === '!delete') {
      const deleteCount = parseInt(args[0], 10);

      if (isNaN(deleteCount) || deleteCount < 1 || deleteCount > 100) {
        return message.reply('Please provide a number between 1 and 100 for the number of messages to delete.');
      }

      try {
        const fetched = await message.channel.messages.fetch({ limit: deleteCount + 1 });
        message.channel.bulkDelete(fetched, true);
      } catch (error) {
        console.log(`Delete ERR: ${error}`);
        message.reply('There was an error trying to delete the messages.');
      }

      return;
    }
  }

  // ... Rest of the code
});
```

This addition will listen for the `!delete` command followed by a number (e.g., `!delete 5`). When the command is detected, it fetches the specified number of messages (plus one to include the command message), and then attempts to delete them using the `bulkDelete()` method. If the provided number is not valid or an error occurs, an error message is sent to the channel.

To create a clean architecture project called "filelynk" using Golang, PostgreSQL, and GORM, you'll want to separate your code into three main layers: domain, repository, and service.

Here's a possible directory structure and some sample code to help you get started:

```
filelynk/
