Yew Agent is a library that provides features similar to Elm's ports for Yew, a Rust framework for building client-side web applications. The 0.2 version of Yew Agent introduces some changes and improvements compared to the previous version.

To use Yew Agent 0.2 in your web application, follow the steps below:

1. First, make sure you have Rust and wasm-pack installed. If you don't, follow the instructions on https://rustwasm.github.io/wasm-pack/installer/ to install wasm-pack.

2. Create a new Yew project:

```bash
cargo new --lib my_yew_agent
cd my_yew_agent
```

3. Add `yew` and `yew_agent` as dependencies in your `Cargo.toml` file:

```toml
[dependencies]
yew = "0.18"
yew_agent = "0.2"
```

4. Define the Agent trait for your application. An agent can be thought of as a service that runs in the background and performs specific tasks or manages state. Create a new file `src/agent.rs` and define the agent struct and its corresponding agent trait:

```rust
use yew_agent::{Agent, Context, HandlerId};

pub enum Request {
    // Add your desired messages/requests for your agent here
}

pub struct MyAgent {
    link: AgentLink<Self>,
}

impl Agent for MyAgent {
    type Reach = Context<Self>;
    type Message = ();
    type Input = Request;
    type Output = ();

    fn create(link: AgentLink<Self>) -> Self {
        MyAgent { link }
    }

    fn update(&mut self, _: Self::Message) {}

    fn handle_input(&mut self, _: Self::Input, _: HandlerId) {
        // Handle the incoming requests here
    }

    fn name_of_resource() -> &'static str {
        "my_agent"
    }
}
```

5. In your main Yew component, use the agent created in the previous step. Open `src/lib.rs` and import the agent and define the component:

```rust
use yew::{html, Component, ComponentLink, Html, ShouldRender};
use yew_agent::Bridge;
use crate::agent::{MyAgent, Request};

mod agent;

pub struct App {
    link: ComponentLink<Self>,
    agent: Box<dyn Bridge<MyAgent>>,
}

pub enum Msg {
    // Add your desired messages for your component here
}

impl Component for App {
    type Message = Msg;
    type Properties = ();

    fn create(_: Self::Properties, link: ComponentLink<Self>) -> Self {
        let agent = MyAgent::bridge(link.callback(|_: ()| Msg::None));
        App { link, agent }
    }

    fn update(&mut self, _: Self::Message) -> ShouldRender {
        // Handle the component messages here
        false
    }

    fn change(&mut self, _: Self::Properties) -> ShouldRender {
        false
    }

    fn view(&self) -> Html {
        html! {
            <>
                // Add your HTML template here
            </>
        }
    }
}
```

6. Update the `src/lib.rs` entry point to render the App component:

```rust
use wasm_bindgen::prelude::*;
use yew::App;

#[wasm_bindgen(start)]
pub fn run_app() {
    App::<crate::App>::new().mount_to_body();
}
```

7. Finally, build the project with `wasm-pack`:

```bash
wasm-pack build --target web
```

Include the `pkg/my_yew_agent_bg.wasm` and `pkg/my_yew_agent.js` files generated by wasm-pack in your HTML file and following the wasm-bindgen instructions.

Now you have a working Yew project with Yew Agent 0.2 available. You can continue to add functionality and components to your application, with agents for background tasks or state management if needed.

